Four places take sizeof(Datum) for the width of a by-value Datum (intcap plan, Phase D).

Under 0010 a Datum is a capability, 16 bytes, holding an 8-byte value; SIZEOF_DATUM
is 8. Where PostgreSQL wrote sizeof(Datum) for the value's width:

- pg_type.c (TypeCreate): an 8-byte by-value type is accepted when its size equals
  sizeof(Datum). The --single setup call stopped after 559 statements at a CREATE
  TYPE: "internal size 8 is invalid for passed-by-value type".
- datum.c (datum_image_hash): hashed all 16 bytes, the capability's metadata half
  with the value.
- outfuncs.c/readfuncs.c (outDatum/readDatum): a stored node tree (a view's rule,
  a column default) carried 16 bytes per by-value constant, the metadata half
  included, where the native tree carries 8.

Each now takes the value (the Datum converted to uint64) and SIZEOF_DATUM bytes of
it, so the hash and the stored text are the native ones on this little-endian
target. The last two change only under __SIZEOF_INTCAP__; pg_type.c's comparison
is the same test natively, where SIZEOF_DATUM is sizeof(Datum).

--- a/src/backend/catalog/pg_type.c
+++ b/src/backend/catalog/pg_type.c
@@ -286,7 +286,8 @@
 								alignment, internalSize)));
 		}
 #if SIZEOF_DATUM == 8
-		else if (internalSize == (int16) sizeof(Datum))
+		/* capstone: the value width, not sizeof(Datum), which is 16 */
+		else if (internalSize == (int16) SIZEOF_DATUM)
 		{
 			if (alignment != TYPALIGN_DOUBLE)
 				ereport(ERROR,
--- a/src/backend/utils/adt/datum.c
+++ b/src/backend/utils/adt/datum.c
@@ -341,7 +341,16 @@
